Circagraph server for macOS (Homebrew)
======================================

Install
-------

    brew install circagraph/tap/circagraph-server
    circagraph-setup

Homebrew installs what the server needs: Node.js 24 (node@24), PostgreSQL 17, GMP and SQLite,
and builds the SQLite converter and the pgmp extension for this Mac (Rust and CMake are needed
only while building). Homebrew's PostgreSQL and the server run as you. circagraph-setup:

  - starts PostgreSQL 17 with `brew services` if it is not running;
  - writes $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/circagraph.env (readable only by you), with
    a new AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY and the database at postgresql://circagraph@/circagraph;
  - creates the circagraph role, which owns the database but is not a superuser (as you
    are in Homebrew's PostgreSQL), and the circagraph database, with the bundled pgmp
    extension. (Installations set up by earlier versions keep connecting as you, as their
    configuration says; setup leaves them so.) Homebrew's PostgreSQL lets anyone on this
    Mac connect as any role (trust authentication); on a shared Mac, set a password for
    yourself and the role (and the role's in DATABASE_URL), and change "trust" to
    "scram-sha-256" in its pg_hba.conf;
  - migrates it (circagraph-migrate) and starts the `circagraph-server` service,
    which listens on 127.0.0.1:5173 and starts again when you log in.

circagraph-setup is safe to run again.

Configure
---------

Edit $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/circagraph.env, then
`brew services restart circagraph-server`:

  - APP_ORIGIN: the public HTTPS origin users reach, without a trailing slash. The server
    refuses a plain http:// origin other than localhost; on a trusted private network
    without a proxy, set ALLOW_INSECURE_ORIGIN=1 and HOST=0.0.0.0 to serve
    http://<name>:5173 directly. The server logs to $(brew --prefix)/var/log/circagraph.log;
    if the website reports "Server misconfigured", the reason is there.
  - RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM for account email, delivered by
    `circagraph-mail`; run it as your launchd agent with `circagraph-agent start mail`
    (below).
  - ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD for the first administrator,
    created by `circagraph-migrate`. The password is used once; to sign in with it again
    (or with a new one written there), run `circagraph-reset-admin-password`.
    Once you have signed in and changed it, empty ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD again, so the file
    holds no working password.
  - Sign-in providers, human verification, quotas: see the comments in the file and
    authentication.md, administration.md in this directory.

Back up AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY apart from the database: without it, enrolled authenticator
secrets cannot be decrypted. Do not change it once accounts exist.

Serve it publicly through an HTTPS reverse proxy (brew install caddy). With Caddy:

    timescale.example {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }

Caddy 2.5 and later replace an incoming X-Forwarded-For with the client address, so set
TRUST_PROXY=1 (with another proxy, only if it does the same; see README.md).

The syslog receiver (syslog.md), `circagraph-syslog`, receives syslog over UDP and TCP on
127.0.0.1:5514 and adds each message to the timelines its routes choose. Set OCH_API_KEY (a
read/write API key from Account -> API keys) in $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/syslog.env
and write the routes to $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/syslog-routes.json, then
`circagraph-agent start syslog`. `cg-import-logs` imports historic log files
(log-import.md): `cg-import-logs --help`.

The GitHub receiver (github.md), `circagraph-github`, receives GitHub's webhooks on
127.0.0.1:5580, behind your HTTPS reverse proxy, and mirrors issues and pull requests into the
timelines its routes choose. Set OCH_API_KEY and GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET in
$(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/github.env and write the routes to
$(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/github-routes.json, then
`circagraph-agent start github`. `cg-import-github` imports what a repository has
already (github.md): `cg-import-github --help`.

Email delivery and the syslog and GitHub receivers run as your launchd agents, as `brew services` runs the
server: started now and whenever you log in, and started again if they stop.

    circagraph-agent start syslog      (or github, or mail)
    circagraph-agent status syslog     running or not, its process and last exit code
    circagraph-agent restart syslog    after editing its configuration
    circagraph-agent stop syslog       stops it, and it no longer starts at login

They log to $(brew --prefix)/var/log/circagraph-syslog.log, circagraph-github.log
and circagraph-mail.log. Their definitions are
~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.circagraph.syslog.plist, .github.plist and .mail.plist.

Upgrade
-------

`brew upgrade circagraph-server` keeps the configuration and the database. Then:

  1. Compare the configuration with this version's template:
         diff -u $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph/circagraph.env \
                 $(brew --prefix)/share/circagraph/circagraph.env.example
  2. Back up:  $(brew --prefix postgresql@17)/bin/pg_dump -h /tmp circagraph > circagraph.sql
  3. circagraph-migrate, which migrates the database and restarts the server, and the
     launchd agents of the mail worker and the syslog and GitHub receivers that you started
     (circagraph-agent start mail|syslog|github), so that they run the new version too.

From openchronology-server
--------------------------

Until 0.30 the formula was openchronology-server. `brew update` and `brew upgrade` move such
an installation to circagraph-server (the tap's formula_renames.json), and the formula moves
its configuration from $(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology to etc/circagraph, and
openchronology.env to circagraph.env, leaving links from the old names. The database and its
role keep their names (openchronology, as DATABASE_URL says), and so does the GitHub
receiver's state in $(brew --prefix)/var/openchronology; use those names in place of
circagraph in the commands above. The old command names (openchronology-setup,
openchronology-migrate, openchronology-agent, och-import-logs, ...) still work.

After the upgrade, run circagraph-migrate once: besides migrating the database, it stops the
openchronology-server service (which brew services no longer lists) and starts
circagraph-server, and moves each launchd agent you started (org.openchronology.mail, .syslog,
.github) to its new name, org.circagraph.*. The logs are now circagraph.log and
circagraph-<worker>.log in $(brew --prefix)/var/log.

Remove
------

Homebrew installed PostgreSQL 17 for the server, so uninstalling the server removes it too,
unless you ask Homebrew to keep it (its databases, in $(brew --prefix)/var/postgresql@17,
remain either way). To keep PostgreSQL and the database running:

    brew install postgresql@17
    circagraph-agent stop syslog; circagraph-agent stop github
    circagraph-agent stop mail
    brew services stop circagraph-server
    brew uninstall circagraph-server

The configuration (including AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY) and the database remain; delete them with
(an installation from openchronology-server: also etc/openchronology and var/openchronology,
and its database and role are openchronology):

    rm -rf $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph $(brew --prefix)/var/circagraph
    $(brew --prefix postgresql@17)/bin/dropdb -h /tmp circagraph
    $(brew --prefix postgresql@17)/bin/dropuser -h /tmp circagraph

Layout
------

  $(brew --prefix)/opt/circagraph-server/libexec   the application and cg-convert
  $(brew --prefix)/etc/circagraph                  configuration (created by setup)
  $(brew --prefix)/share/circagraph                configuration templates of this version
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.circagraph.*.plist    the agents circagraph-agent started
  $(brew --prefix)/lib/postgresql@17, share/postgresql@17   pgmp
